"AIConfigurator: Lightning-Fast Configuration Optimization for Multi-Framework LLM Serving (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06288v1)\nciteturn26835view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06288v1\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 538\nL109: InferenceSession: InferenceSession will then iterate over all the candidate serving configurations, and for each candidate, it will estimate key performance metrics by combining iteration-level modeling with operator-level performance data queried directly from the PerfDatabase.\nL110: \nL111:   * •\nL112: Pareto Analyzer: Afterwards, the Pareto analyzer will filter and rank all the valid serving configurations based on the performance projections generated in the previous stage, and output the top-ranked configurations with comprehensive performance projections.\nL113: \nL114:   * •\nL115: Generator: Finally, AIConfigurator’s generator module can directly convert the serving recommendations identified by the Pareto analyzer into version compatible launch file for any one of the inference engines among TensorRT-LLM, vLLM and SGLang, automatically setting the optimal serving flags such as --enable_cuda_graph, --kv_cache_free_gpu_mem_fraction and --enable_chunked_context.\nL116: Serving frameworks like NVIDIA Dynamo can also directly leverage the launch file to set up an optimally configured inference server.\nL117: ### 4.2 Performance Modeling\nL118: \nL119: Instead of directly estimating the system throughput and generation speed, AIConfigurator derives these two metrics from the perspective of an average request arriving at the server operating at a steady state of concurrency.\nL120: \nL121:  | $$\\text{Generation Speed}=1000/TPOT$$  |  | (1)\nL122:  | $$\\begin{split}\\text{System Throughput}&=\\frac{1000}{TTFT+(OSL-1)\\times TPOT}\\\\\nL123: &*\\text{Batch Size}\\\\\nL124: &*OSL\\\\\nL125: &/\\text{Total Number of GPU}\\end{split}$$  |  | (2)\nL126: Both TTFT(Time to First Token) and TPOT(Time Per Output Token) are measured in milliseconds. OSL(output sequence length) is treated as a fixed value provided by the user-supplied workload descriptor. As for Batch size, AIConfigurator will sweep over a range of pre-defined values when estimating the performance for a given serving configuration.\nL127: In order to estimate TPOT and TTFT efficiently, we first consider the full life cycle of a typical request being processed by an inference server. In the context of LLM serving, any given request will go through two distinct processing phases:\nL128: The prefill/context phase processes the entire user input prompt, computing and storing the associated KV cache before producing the first output token. This phase is normally compute intensive, and fused multi-head attention style kernels like FlashAttention [cite54†8 ] are usually utilized in this phase to accelerate the attention computation. Additionally, context chunking [cite38†5 ] can also be employed to split prefill of long input prompts into multiple iteration steps.\nL129: The decode/generation phase generates the remaining output tokens one at a time in a step-by-step auto-regressive fashion. Newly generated tokens are utilized as the input for the subsequent step, and cached key-value pairs are used to avoid recomputing key-value pairs for the past tokens. This phase is normally memory intensive and specialized kernels like XQA [cite31†2 ] are utilized in this phase.\nL130: While prefill and decode constitute the fundamental computational stages of LLM inference, real-world performance depends heavily on how the serving engine orchestrates them. A naive abstraction is insufficient to capture the nuances of different scheduling strategies. Therefore, AIConfigurator explicitly models three distinct serving modes—Static, Aggregated, and Disaggregated—each of which handles resource contention and phase interleaving differently.\nL131: We first define the baseline behavior under the static mode.\nL132: Algorithm 1 Static Mode Inference Performance Estimation\nL133: \nL134: 1: $ISL$ (Input Length), $OSL$ (Output Length)\nL135: \nL136: 2: $B$ (Batch Size), $P$ (Prefix Length)\nL137: \nL138: 3: Stride $S_{stride}$ (Default: 32)\nL139: \nL140: 4: Function $\\textsc{GetStepLatency}(batch\\_size,$\nL141: \nL142: 5:                $seq\\_len,phase)$\nL143: \nL144: 6: Phase 1: Context Latency (TTFT)\nL145: \nL146: 7: $ISL_{eff}\\leftarrow ISL-P$\nL147: \nL148: 8: $\\triangleright$ Calculate latency for processing the input prompt\nL149: \nL150: 9: $\\text{TTFT}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetStepLatency}(B,ISL_{eff},\\text{'prefill'})$\nL151: 10: Phase 2: Generation Latency (Total Generation Time)\nL152: \nL153: 11: $T_{gen}\\leftarrow 0$\nL154: \nL155: 12: if $OSL>1$ then\nL156: \nL157: 13:   $k\\leftarrow 0$\nL158: \nL159: 14:   while $k<OSL-1$ do\nL160: \nL161: 15:    $S_{seq}\\leftarrow ISL+k+1$ $\\triangleright$ Current total sequence length\nL162: \nL163: 16:    $\\triangleright$ Query latency for a single decode step at current length\nL164: \nL165: 17:    $T_{step}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetStepLatency}(B,S_{seq},\\text{'decode'})$\nL166: \nL167: 18:    $R\\leftarrow\\min(S_{stride},OSL-1-k)$ $\\triangleright$ Interpolate for next R tokens\nL168: 19:    $T_{gen}\\leftarrow T_{gen}+(T_{step}\\times R)$\nL169: \nL170: 20:    $k\\leftarrow k+S_{stride}$\nL171: \nL172: 21:   end while\nL173: \nL174: 22: end if\nL175: \nL176: 23: Phase 3: TPOT Calculation\nL177: \nL178: 24: if $OSL>1$ then\nL179: \nL180: 25:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow T_{gen}/(OSL-1)$\nL181: \nL182: 26: else\nL183: \nL184: 27:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow 0$\nL185: \nL186: 28: end if\nL187: \nL188: 29: return $\\text{TTFT},\\text{TPOT}$\nL189: #### 4.2.1 Static Mode\nL190: \nL191: In the static serving mode, as depicted in Figure cite55†3 (A), the workload is processed in a strictly sequential manner with a fixed batch size. In this regime, TTFT is equivalent to the latency of the prefill phase. TPOT is approximated by averaging the latency of the subsequent decoding steps required to generate the entirety of the output sequence.\nL192: Algorithm cite56†1 outlines the procedure for calculating these metrics. It employs a stride-based optimization (Step cite57†18 ) to reduce the computational overhead of estimating generation latency, interpolating costs over intervals rather than querying the database for every token step.\nL193: #### 4.2.2 Aggregated Mode\nL194: \nL195: Also known as inflight batching or continuous batching [cite37†24 ], this serving mode is distinguished by its ability to mix prefill and decode steps from different requests within a single inference iteration, as shown in Figure cite55†3 (B). This flexibility significantly improves GPU resource utilization compared to static serving, leading to higher system throughput.\nL196: Our performance model, detailed in Algorithm cite58†2 , approximates this behavior by dividing execution into two distinct stages:\nL197: Mixed Phase: This phase represents the steady-state operation of a continuous batching system where prefill and decode requests run concurrently. The scheduler prioritizes utilizing the available context capacity ($C_{ctx}$) to process prefill requests. By default a prefill request will contain a ISL number of tokens, and context chunking can be optionally enabled to split full ISL number of context tokens into multiple prefill requests.\nL198: The remaining batch slots are allocated to decoding tasks ($N_{mix}^{gen}$). Crucially, when the prefill workload is heavy (context processing time exceeds generation time), our model (Algorithm cite58†2 , lines 6-10) throttles the number of concurrent decode streams using a rate-matching heuristic. This prevents the \"starvation\" scenario where decode requests finish faster than new requests can be prefilled. The step latency ($L_{mix}$) in this phase is dominated by the compute-intensive prefill attention.\nL199: Generation-Only Phase: This phase models the tail end of a workload or periods of low arrival rate where the prefill queue has been drained. The system transitions to a pure decoding regime, dedicating all batch slots ($B$) to autoregressive generation. The step latency ($L_{gen}$) here is significantly lower, typically bounded by memory bandwidth rather than compute.\nL200: We estimate TTFT based on the latency of the Mixed Phase, incorporating an empirical correction factor ($F_{corr}$) modeled as a piecewise linear function. This factor accounts for base scheduling overhead (constant term), proportional queuing delay as the context backlog grows, and a saturation limit to reflect system-level admission controls. For TPOT, we employ a weighted average of latencies from both phases.\nL201: Notably, we apply a small offset (3 steps) to the mixed phase duration when calculating weights (Algorithm cite58†2 , Step 5). This heuristic filters out scheduling jitter often observed in the initial steps of a batch, providing a more robust estimate of steady-state decoding performance. This approach models the non-linear interference between prefill and decode operations without requiring a full discrete-event simulation.\nL202: Algorithm 2 Aggregated Mode (Continuous Batching) Performance Estimation\nL203: \nL204: 1: $ISL,OSL$\nL205: \nL206: 2: $B$ (Batch Size), $C_{ctx}$ (Context Token Capacity)\nL207: \nL208: 3: Helper Functions:\nL209: \nL210: 4:  $\\textsc{GetMixLat}(N_{ctx},N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL211: \nL212: 5:  $\\textsc{GetGenLat}(N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL213: \nL214: 6: Step 1: Phase Duration (in Steps)\nL215: \nL216: 7: $T_{total\\_ctx}\\leftarrow\\lceil(ISL\\times B)/C_{ctx}\\rceil$ $\\triangleright$ Total steps to process all context\nL217: \nL218: 8: Step 2: Workload Distribution (Steps & Tokens)\nL219: \nL220: 9: if $B>1$ then\nL221: 10:   if $T_{total\\_ctx}\\geq OSL$ then\nL222: \nL223: 11:    $\\triangleright$ Context dominates; generation slots are limited\nL224: \nL225: 12:    $T_{mix}\\leftarrow T_{total\\_ctx}$\nL226: \nL227: 13:    $T_{gen}\\leftarrow 0$\nL228: \nL229: 14:    $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx}$ $\\triangleright$ Tokens per step\nL230: \nL231: 15:    $N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow\\max(1,\\lfloor B/(T_{total\\_ctx}/OSL)\\rfloor)$ $\\triangleright$ Tokens per step\nL232: \nL233: 16:   else\nL234: \nL235: 17:    $\\triangleright$ Standard continuous batching\nL236: \nL237: 18:    $T_{mix}\\leftarrow T_{total\\_ctx}$\nL238: 19:    $T_{gen}\\leftarrow OSL-T_{mix}$\nL239: \nL240: 20:    $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx}$\nL241: \nL242: 21:    $N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow B-\\lceil C_{ctx}/ISL\\rceil$ $\\triangleright$ Fill remaining slots\nL243: \nL244: 22:    assert $N_{mix}^{gen}\\geq 1$\nL245: \nL246: 23:   end if\nL247: \nL248: 24: else\nL249: \nL250: 25:   $\\triangleright$ Single Batch ($B=1$)\nL251: \nL252: 26:   $T_{mix}\\leftarrow 1,\\quad T_{gen}\\leftarrow OSL-1$\nL253: \nL254: 27:   $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx},\\quad N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow 0$\nL255: \nL256: 28: end if\nL257: \nL258: 29: Step 3: Latency Calculation\nL259: 30: $L_{mix}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetMixLat}(N_{mix}^{ctx},N_{mix}^{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL260: \nL261: 31: $L_{gen}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetGenLat}(B,ISL,OSL)$\nL262: \nL263: 32: Step 4: TTFT Estimation\nL264: \nL265: 33: $F_{corr}\\leftarrow\\min\\left(2+\\frac{T_{total\\_ctx}-3}{20},4\\right)$\nL266: \nL267: 34: $\\text{TTFT}\\leftarrow L_{mix}\\times\\lceil ISL/C_{ctx}\\rceil\\times F_{corr}$\nL268: \nL269: 35: Step 5: TPOT Estimation\nL270: \nL271: 36: $T^{\\prime}_{mix}\\leftarrow\\max(1,T_{mix}-3)$\nL272: \nL273: 37: if $B>1$ then\nL274: 38:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow\\frac{L_{mix}\\times T^{\\prime}_{mix}+L_{gen}\\times T_{gen}}{T^{\\prime}_{mix}+T_{gen}}$\nL275: \nL276: 39: else\nL277: \nL278: 40:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow L_{gen}$\nL279: \nL280: 41: end if\nL281: \nL282: 42: return $\\text{TTFT},\\text{TPOT}$\nL283: #### 4.2.3 Disaggregated Mode\nL284: \nL285: Algorithm 3 Disaggregated Mode Performance Estimation\nL286: \nL287: 1: Candidate Configs: $C_{pre}$ (Prefill), $C_{dec}$ (Decode)\nL288: \nL289: 2: Constraints: Max TTFT ($L_{limit}^{TTFT}$), Max TPOT ($L_{limit}^{TPOT}$)\nL290: \nL291: 3: Valid Total GPU Counts: $G_{valid}$\nL292: \nL293: 4: Degradation Factors: $\\alpha_{pre}=0.9,\\alpha_{dec}=0.92$\nL294: \nL295: 5: TTFT Correction Factor: $\\beta_{TTFT}=1.8$\nL296: \nL297: 6: Step 1: Filter Candidates by Latency\nL298: \nL299: 7: $\\triangleright$ Filter prefill configs ($c_{pre}$) and decode configs ($c_{dec}$)\nL300: 8: $C^{\\prime}_{pre}\\leftarrow\\{c\\in C_{pre}\\mid(L_{pre}(c)\\times\\beta_{TTFT})\\leq L_{limit}^{TTFT}\\}$\nL301: \nL302: 9: $C^{\\prime}_{dec}\\leftarrow\\{c\\in C_{dec}\\mid L_{dec}(c)\\leq L_{limit}^{TPOT}\\}$\nL303: \nL304: 10: Step 2: Rate Matching (Find Optimal $x,y$)\nL305: \nL306: 11: $BestConfig\\leftarrow\\emptyset,\\quad MaxThru_{GPU}\\leftarrow 0$\nL307: \nL308: 12: for $c_{dec}\\in C^{\\prime}_{dec}$ do\nL309: \nL310: 13:   for $c_{pre}\\in C^{\\prime}_{pre}$ do\nL311: \nL312: 14:    $Thru_{pre}\\leftarrow\\text{SeqThroughput}(c_{pre})$\nL313: \nL314: 15:    $Thru_{dec}\\leftarrow\\text{SeqThroughput}(c_{dec})$\nL315: 16:    $G_{pre}\\leftarrow\\text{GPUs}(c_{pre}),\\quad G_{dec}\\leftarrow\\text{GPUs}(c_{dec})$\nL316: \nL317: 17:    $\\triangleright$ Sweep worker counts $x$ (prefill) and $y$ (decode)\nL318: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAIConfigurator: Lightning-Fast Configuration Optimization for Multi-Framework LLM Serving (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06288v1)\nciteturn26835view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06288v1\",\"lineno\":305}); Total lines: 538\nL200: We estimate TTFT based on the latency of the Mixed Phase, incorporating an empirical correction factor ($F_{corr}$) modeled as a piecewise linear function. This factor accounts for base scheduling overhead (constant term), proportional queuing delay as the context backlog grows, and a saturation limit to reflect system-level admission controls. For TPOT, we employ a weighted average of latencies from both phases.\nL201: Notably, we apply a small offset (3 steps) to the mixed phase duration when calculating weights (Algorithm cite58†2 , Step 5). This heuristic filters out scheduling jitter often observed in the initial steps of a batch, providing a more robust estimate of steady-state decoding performance. This approach models the non-linear interference between prefill and decode operations without requiring a full discrete-event simulation.\nL202: Algorithm 2 Aggregated Mode (Continuous Batching) Performance Estimation\nL203: \nL204: 1: $ISL,OSL$\nL205: \nL206: 2: $B$ (Batch Size), $C_{ctx}$ (Context Token Capacity)\nL207: \nL208: 3: Helper Functions:\nL209: \nL210: 4:  $\\textsc{GetMixLat}(N_{ctx},N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL211: \nL212: 5:  $\\textsc{GetGenLat}(N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL213: \nL214: 6: Step 1: Phase Duration (in Steps)\nL215: \nL216: 7: $T_{total\\_ctx}\\leftarrow\\lceil(ISL\\times B)/C_{ctx}\\rceil$ $\\triangleright$ Total steps to process all context\nL217: \nL218: 8: Step 2: Workload Distribution (Steps & Tokens)\nL219: \nL220: 9: if $B>1$ then\nL221: 10:   if $T_{total\\_ctx}\\geq OSL$ then\nL222: \nL223: 11:    $\\triangleright$ Context dominates; generation slots are limited\nL224: \nL225: 12:    $T_{mix}\\leftarrow T_{total\\_ctx}$\nL226: \nL227: 13:    $T_{gen}\\leftarrow 0$\nL228: \nL229: 14:    $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx}$ $\\triangleright$ Tokens per step\nL230: \nL231: 15:    $N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow\\max(1,\\lfloor B/(T_{total\\_ctx}/OSL)\\rfloor)$ $\\triangleright$ Tokens per step\nL232: \nL233: 16:   else\nL234: \nL235: 17:    $\\triangleright$ Standard continuous batching\nL236: \nL237: 18:    $T_{mix}\\leftarrow T_{total\\_ctx}$\nL238: 19:    $T_{gen}\\leftarrow OSL-T_{mix}$\nL239: \nL240: 20:    $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx}$\nL241: \nL242: 21:    $N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow B-\\lceil C_{ctx}/ISL\\rceil$ $\\triangleright$ Fill remaining slots\nL243: \nL244: 22:    assert $N_{mix}^{gen}\\geq 1$\nL245: \nL246: 23:   end if\nL247: \nL248: 24: else\nL249: \nL250: 25:   $\\triangleright$ Single Batch ($B=1$)\nL251: \nL252: 26:   $T_{mix}\\leftarrow 1,\\quad T_{gen}\\leftarrow OSL-1$\nL253: \nL254: 27:   $N_{mix}^{ctx}\\leftarrow C_{ctx},\\quad N_{mix}^{gen}\\leftarrow 0$\nL255: \nL256: 28: end if\nL257: \nL258: 29: Step 3: Latency Calculation\nL259: 30: $L_{mix}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetMixLat}(N_{mix}^{ctx},N_{mix}^{gen},ISL,OSL)$\nL260: \nL261: 31: $L_{gen}\\leftarrow\\textsc{GetGenLat}(B,ISL,OSL)$\nL262: \nL263: 32: Step 4: TTFT Estimation\nL264: \nL265: 33: $F_{corr}\\leftarrow\\min\\left(2+\\frac{T_{total\\_ctx}-3}{20},4\\right)$\nL266: \nL267: 34: $\\text{TTFT}\\leftarrow L_{mix}\\times\\lceil ISL/C_{ctx}\\rceil\\times F_{corr}$\nL268: \nL269: 35: Step 5: TPOT Estimation\nL270: \nL271: 36: $T^{\\prime}_{mix}\\leftarrow\\max(1,T_{mix}-3)$\nL272: \nL273: 37: if $B>1$ then\nL274: 38:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow\\frac{L_{mix}\\times T^{\\prime}_{mix}+L_{gen}\\times T_{gen}}{T^{\\prime}_{mix}+T_{gen}}$\nL275: \nL276: 39: else\nL277: \nL278: 40:   $\\text{TPOT}\\leftarrow L_{gen}$\nL279: \nL280: 41: end if\nL281: \nL282: 42: return $\\text{TTFT},\\text{TPOT}$\nL283: #### 4.2.3 Disaggregated Mode\nL284: \nL285: Algorithm 3 Disaggregated Mode Performance Estimation\nL286: \nL287: 1: Candidate Configs: $C_{pre}$ (Prefill), $C_{dec}$ (Decode)\nL288: \nL289: 2: Constraints: Max TTFT ($L_{limit}^{TTFT}$), Max TPOT ($L_{limit}^{TPOT}$)\nL290: \nL291: 3: Valid Total GPU Counts: $G_{valid}$\nL292: \nL293: 4: Degradation Factors: $\\alpha_{pre}=0.9,\\alpha_{dec}=0.92$\nL294: \nL295: 5: TTFT Correction Factor: $\\beta_{TTFT}=1.8$\nL296: \nL297: 6: Step 1: Filter Candidates by Latency\nL298: \nL299: 7: $\\triangleright$ Filter prefill configs ($c_{pre}$) and decode configs ($c_{dec}$)\nL300: 8: $C^{\\prime}_{pre}\\leftarrow\\{c\\in C_{pre}\\mid(L_{pre}(c)\\times\\beta_{TTFT})\\leq L_{limit}^{TTFT}\\}$\nL301: \nL302: 9: $C^{\\prime}_{dec}\\leftarrow\\{c\\in C_{dec}\\mid L_{dec}(c)\\leq L_{limit}^{TPOT}\\}$\nL303: \nL304: 10: Step 2: Rate Matching (Find Optimal $x,y$)\nL305: \nL306: 11: $BestConfig\\leftarrow\\emptyset,\\quad MaxThru_{GPU}\\leftarrow 0$\nL307: \nL308: 12: for $c_{dec}\\in C^{\\prime}_{dec}$ do\nL309: \nL310: 13:   for $c_{pre}\\in C^{\\prime}_{pre}$ do\nL311: \nL312: 14:    $Thru_{pre}\\leftarrow\\text{SeqThroughput}(c_{pre})$\nL313: \nL314: 15:    $Thru_{dec}\\leftarrow\\text{SeqThroughput}(c_{dec})$\nL315: 16:    $G_{pre}\\leftarrow\\text{GPUs}(c_{pre}),\\quad G_{dec}\\leftarrow\\text{GPUs}(c_{dec})$\nL316: \nL317: 17:    $\\triangleright$ Sweep worker counts $x$ (prefill) and $y$ (decode)\nL318: \nL319: 18:    for $x\\in[1,32],\\ y\\in[1,64]$ do\nL320: \nL321: 19:      $G_{total}\\leftarrow x\\cdot G_{pre}+y\\cdot G_{dec}$\nL322: \nL323: 20:      if $G_{total}\\notin G_{valid}$ then continue\nL324: \nL325: 21:      end if\nL326: \nL327: 22:      $R_{pre}\\leftarrow Thru_{pre}\\cdot x\\cdot\\alpha_{pre}$\nL328: \nL329: 23:      $R_{dec}\\leftarrow Thru_{dec}\\cdot y\\cdot\\alpha_{dec}$\nL330: 24:      $R_{sys}\\leftarrow\\min(R_{pre},R_{dec})$ $\\triangleright$ System Rate\nL331: \nL332: 25:      $Thru_{GPU}\\leftarrow R_{sys}/G_{total}$\nL333: \nL334: 26:      if $Thru_{GPU}>MaxThru_{GPU}$ then\nL335: \nL336: 27:       $MaxThru_{GPU}\\leftarrow Thru_{GPU}$\nL337: \nL338: 28:       $BestConfig\\leftarrow\\{\\text{TTFT}:L_{pre}(c_{pre}),\\text{TPOT}:L_{dec}(c_{dec}),\\dots\\}$\nL339: \nL340: 29:      end if\nL341: \nL342: 30:    end for\nL343: \nL344: 31:   end for\nL345: \nL346: 32: end for\nL347: \nL348: 33: return $BestConfig$\nL349: In contrast to the static and aggregated modes, the Disaggregated Mode employs two distinct worker pools, each dedicated to a specific phase of LLM inference. As illustrated in Figure cite55†3 (C), incoming requests are first processed by dedicated prefill workers. Upon completion of the prefill phase, the computed key-value (KV) cache and intermediate states are transferred to decode workers, which generate subsequent tokens in an autoregressive manner.\nL350: This decoupling offers significant architectural advantages: it eliminates prefill-decode interference and allows each pool to employ distinct model parallelism strategies specialized for its specific workload characteristics—optimizing compute-bound prefill and memory-bound decoding independently. This separation has been shown to significantly improve overall system Goodput in prior studies [cite29†28 ].\nL351: From a modeling perspective, AIConfigurator estimates the performance of a disaggregated system in two stages. Firstly, it independently sweeps the search space for prefill and decode configurations, treating each candidate as an isolated static instance and estimating its base latency using Algorithm cite56†1 . It applies a correction factor ($\\beta_{TTFT}$) to the prefill latency to account for the KV-cache transmission overhead inherent to disaggregated architectures.\nL352: Secondly, it constructs valid composite servers—denoted as $(x)P(y)D$, where $x$ and $y$ represent the number of prefill and decode instances, respectively—through a rate-matching process. The algorithm identifies the optimal configuration by maximizing the effective per-GPU throughput ($Thru_{GPU}$), derived from the system rate $R_{sys}$:\nL353: \nL354:  | $$R_{sys}=\\min(R_{pre},R_{dec})$$  |\nL355: where $R$ represents the request rate (requests per second) of the respective worker pools, discounted by interference factors $\\alpha$. Once the optimal $(x)P(y)D$ configuration is identified, the system’s TTFT is derived from the latency of the selected prefill workers pool (including transfer overhead), while TPOT is determined by the latency of the decode workers pool. Algorithm cite59†3 details this optimization procedure.\nL356: ### 4.3 Iteration-level Modeling\nL357: The effectiveness of all three of the algorithms introduced thus far depends on how accurately we can predict the latency of a given inference iteration step.\nL358: For instance, in Algorithm cite56†1 , we rely on the latency data of prefill-only step and decode-step acquired via $\\textsc{GetStepLatency}(batch\\_size,seq\\_len,phase)$ to perform further estimation, while in Algorithm cite58†2 , besides decode-only step ($\\textsc{GetGenLat}(N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$), we must also obtain accurate latency measurement for the mixed step ($\\textsc{GetMixLat}(N_{ctx},N_{gen},ISL,OSL)$) in order to move forward.\nL359: Therefore, aiming at establishing a fast and robust way of estimating the latency of a given iteration step, AIConfigurator approaches this issue by fully exploiting the decomposable nature of the modern LLM inference.\nL360: #### 4.3.1 Decompose Iteration Into Operators\nL361: As modern LLMs are composed of repetitive transformer layers, any inference iteration step can then be modeled as running a fixed sequence of operators for a number of times, typically depending on the number of layers that the model possesses.\nL362: Introducing modern parallel strategies does not alter this fundamental property except for inserting a few well-defined communication operators at fixed positions of the iteration’s execution path and scaling down the compute operators by sharding inputs across multiple compute devices.\nL363: For instance, Figure cite60†4 depicts a typical composition of operators while performing inference with an MoE model. The entire inference step essentially amounts to running 4 types of operators, namely embedding, GEMM, Attention(prefill or decode, depending on the phase) and MoE for a number of times.\nL364: If expert parallelism is added to the mix, it will scale down the size of MoE operator while adding a pair of communication operators, and the exact pair shall depend on the inference engine backend used in production.\nL365: Given the above observations, in AIConfigurator, we model the latency of an inference iteration step by aggregating the performance profiles of its constituent operators.\nL366: \nL367: cite61†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: A step of LLM inference can be decomposed into repeated execution of a few key operators. For instance, the inference step of a typical MoE model normally involves the above depicted operators, and how expert parallelism is implemented depends on the specific backend used.\nL368: ### 4.4 Operator Database\nL369: \nL370: AIConfigurator constructs a performance database through offline profiling on actual GPU hardware, currently supporting TensorRT-LLM, vLLM, and SGLang.\nL371: Database Coverage.\nL372: The database includes: (1) GEMM operations parameterized by dimensions $(M,N,K)$ and quantization (FP16, FP8, INT8, INT4); (2) Attention operations for both compute-bound context attention and memory-bound generation attention, supporting MHA [cite62†22 ], GQA [cite63†7 ], and MLA [cite64†9 ]; (3) Communication primitives including AllReduce, AllGather, AllToAll, and point-to-point transfers across message sizes and GPU counts; (4) MoE operations with dispatch/combine [cite65†25 ] patterns; and (5) Hardware specifications (memory bandwidth, compute throughput, interconnect bandwidth).\nL373: Data Collection. We combine three strategies: exhaustive profiling sweeps parameters (batch size, sequence length, hidden dimension) with framework-native tools ($\\sim$30 GPU-hours per platform-framework pair); interpolation estimates latencies for intermediate configurations using profiled data points; and Speed-of-Light estimation provides analytical bounds via roofline models for unprofiled operators.\nL374: #### 4.4.1 Power Law Correction for MoE\nL375: \nL376: MoE operator performance depends heavily on token distribution. Prior works [cite66†10 , cite67†14 ] show that during inference, certain experts receive disproportionately more tokens—observations from Qwen3-235B indicate $\\sim$70% of compute is handled by only 20% of active experts. To account for this imbalance, AIConfigurator implements a controlled token assignment procedure that emulates power-law distributions observed in production (Figure cite68†5 ).\nL377: Step 1: Sample Expert Load Weights. We generate a load profile by sampling $E$ weights (one per expert) from a power-law distribution. Using inverse transform sampling with $U\\sim\\text{Uniform}(0,1)$, each raw weight is computed as:\nL378: \nL379:  | $$x_{i}=\\left[(x_{\\max}^{1-\\alpha}-x_{\\min}^{1-\\alpha})\\cdot U+x_{\\min}^{1-\\alpha}\\right]^{\\frac{1}{1-\\alpha}}$$  |  | (3)\n"
